{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["And He said: <b>\"Six days you shall labor and do all your work.\"</b> This verse is entirely superfluous, and furthermore that it begins with עֲבוֹדָה (labor) and concludes with מְלָאכָה (work), and furthermore that the expression עֲבוֹדָה requires explanation, for there is no slave except in combination with a master — and one who labors for himself, who is his master? And in our composition \"Ollot Ephraim,\" discourse 261, we explained that \"תַּעֲבֹד\" (you shall labor) speaks of the service of HaShem, blessed be He, and He said that all six days you shall first perform the service of HaShem, and afterward you may also do all your work, for Torah is good when accompanied by labor. But the seventh day is entirely for HaShem your Elohim — you shall not do any work of your own in it, but the service of HaShem, blessed be He, you shall perform even on Shabbat. And He said \"שֵׁשֶׁת\" (six of) and did not say \"שִׁשָּׁה\" (six), to indicate that you need not do your work for six complete days, for behold one must add from the profane onto the holy, and \"שֵׁשֶׁת\" is like \"שְׁלֹשֶׁת יָמִים\" (three of days), which means three belonging to days." "And what is said in the first Decalogue, \"for in six days HaShem made...\" etc., and in the second it is written (Deuteronomy 5:15) \"and you shall remember that you were a slave...\" etc. — all is one reason, according to the commentators who say that the reason for every one of the plagues of Egypt was to proclaim the belief in the creation of the world anew, as the Mahariza, of blessed memory, wrote, and so we wrote above in parashat Va'era: for through those signs they would also believe in the creation of the world anew. If so, these two reasons are entirely one. And nearby this will be explained in another way. And the commentators spoke further on resolving this question; seek it and you shall find it." "<b>Remember the Sabbath day to sanctify it.</b> We have seen fit to give good reason and understanding for the variations found between the first Decalogue and the latter: (1) In the first it is written \"זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת\" (Exodus 20:8), and in the latter it is written \"שָׁמוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת\" (Deuteronomy 5:12). (2) In the first it is not written \"כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוְּךָ HaShem אֱלֹהֶיךָ\" (as HaShem your Elohim commanded you), and in the latter it is written regarding Shabbat and regarding honoring father and mother, \"כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוְּךָ HaShem אֱלֹהֶיךָ\" (Deuteronomy 5:12, 16). (3) In the first He gave a reason for observing Shabbat, \"for in six days HaShem made...\" etc. (Exodus 20:11), and in the latter He gave another reason, \"and you shall remember that you were a slave...\" etc. (Deuteronomy 5:15). (4) In the first He did not mention ox and donkey, and in the latter He said \"your ox and your donkey and all your livestock\" (Deuteronomy 5:14). (5) In the first it is written \"לְמַעַן יַאֲרִיכוּן יָמֶיךָ\" (Exodus 20:12), and in the latter He added \"לְמַעַן יִיטַב לָךְ\" (Deuteronomy 5:16). (6) In the first it is written \"לֹא תַחְמֹד\" (Exodus 20:14), and in the latter He added \"לֹא תִתְאַוֶּה\" (Deuteronomy 5:18). (7) In the first He did not mention \"שָׂדֵהוּ\" (his field), and in the latter He added \"שָׂדֵהוּ\" (Deuteronomy 5:18). (8) In the first \"בֵּית רֵעֶךָ\" (your fellow's house) preceded \"אֵשֶׁת רֵעֶךָ\" (your fellow's wife) (Exodus 20:14), and in the latter \"אֵשֶׁת רֵעֶךָ\" preceded \"בֵּית רֵעֶךָ\" (Deuteronomy 5:18). (9) In the first it is written \"עֵד שֶׁקֶר\" (false witness) (Exodus 20:13), and in the latter He said \"עֵד שָׁוְא\" (vain witness) (Deuteronomy 5:17). (10) In the first \"לֹא תִנְאָף, לֹא תִגְנֹב\" (Exodus 20:13) are all without a vav, and in the latter they are all with a vav (Deuteronomy 5:17)." "Since the commentators of the Torah have already gone out to gather scattered reasons, and have not found a single way that suffices for all the questions mentioned — is it not worth hearing one short proposal, drawn from the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni, Deuteronomy 33, 951), that the Holy One, blessed be He, went around to all the nations to see if they wished to receive the Torah, and Esau did not want to receive it on account of \"You shall not murder,\" and Ishmael on account of \"You shall not steal,\" and Ammon and Moab on account of \"You shall not commit adultery\" — hear from this that at first all the nations heard all ten commandments, and once each nation said \"I did not wish to take it,\" from then on He gave it to Israel. And therefore all these variations between the first Decalogue and the latter are found, for the first commandments were spoken in the language that HaShem made audible to all the nations, even though they were spoken directly to Israel, as is implied by the expression \"who brought you out of the land of Egypt\" — in any case, had the nations wanted, they too would have accepted upon themselves and upon their seed all the matters that HaShem commanded Israel. By way of parable: Isaac commanded Jacob not to take a wife from the daughters of Canaan; \"And Esau saw that the daughters of Canaan were displeasing in the eyes of Isaac his father, and Esau went to Ishmael...\" etc. — even though Isaac did not command Esau at all, nevertheless after he saw that his father's mind was not pleased with the daughters of Canaan, he too wished to fulfill his father's will in order to find favor in his eyes. So too all the nations who heard all the commandments that HaShem commanded Israel, and saw that HaShem desired these commandments — by justice they too should have accepted them, to observe and to do all that HaShem commanded Israel. And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke the first commandments in such a manner that the nations would find no opening of the mouth to argue and to protest against them in any respect. And therefore the first commandments were spoken with voices of voices, and the voice went from one end of the world to the other, in order that all the nations would hear and accept from them every matter fitting for all of them equally. And since they refused to accept them on account of those particular commandments that were against them, such as \"You shall not murder,\" \"You shall not commit adultery\" — therefore afterward He spoke the second commandments privately to Israel alone." "And with this all these variations are resolved. In the first He said \"Remember the Sabbath day.\" He did not wish to say \"Observe the Sabbath day,\" so as not to give the nations an opening of the mouth to say: How does He command us regarding the guarding against all labor — was it not already said to the children of Noah, \"day and night shall not cease\" (Genesis 8:22), and our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 58b) learned from this: a gentile who rests is liable to death. Therefore \"Remember the Sabbath day\" was said — to say that even one who is not in the category of guarding (shemirah), at least is in the category of remembering (zikhirah), for all the nations are obligated to remember the Sabbath day in order to fix in their hearts the belief in the creation of the world anew, which gives the trustworthy testimony of HaShem to the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, for included in the seven commandments of the children of Noah is that they shall not serve idolatry. And even though the nations cannot accept the command of \"you shall not do any labor,\" nevertheless they are able to accept upon themselves the commandment of remembering, which they too are obligated in — that the creation of the world anew be before their eyes as a remembrance. And for this reason it was necessary to give a reason in the first commandments, \"for in six days HaShem made...\" etc., for this is a reason to remember the creation of the world anew. And had He given the reason \"and you shall remember that you were a slave...\" etc., the nations would have said: We were not slaves in Egypt; therefore we do not need to accept the Sabbath. For this reason He gave them the reason \"for in six days HaShem made...\" etc., for this reason suffices for both of them, for Israel and for the nations. And do not answer me from what is said, \"I am HaShem your Elohim who brought you out of the land of Egypt\" (Exodus 20:2) — perhaps the nations would say: He did not bring us out of Egypt, and therefore we are not obligated to accept Him as Elohim. The answer to this: the meaning is not that on account of that exodus they are obligated to accept Him, for even without that they are obligated to accept Him on the grounds that He created heaven and earth and all that is in them. And what He hung the matter on this exodus was to say that even this exodus alone would suffice to enslave you to Me. And this is what Rashi explained: \"The exodus is sufficient that you should be enslaved to Me.\" From the fact that he said \"the exodus is sufficient\" — hear from this that this is not the essential reason." "But the second set of Decalogue utterances, which were spoken to Israel alone, were spoken in the language of שָׁמוֹר. For they are obligated in both remembrance (זְכִירָה) and observance (שְׁמִירָה) regarding all manner of labor; therefore He gave the reason: ״וְזָכַרְתָּ כִּי עֶבֶד הָיִיתָ״ etc. (Deuteronomy 5:15), for this is the reason for the cessation from labor." "And there are those who say that what is hidden in זָכוֹר — when you write זַי״ן כִַ״ף רֵישׁ סוֹף as the initial letters — spells נֶפֶשׁ, and what is hidden in שָׁמוֹר — when you write שִי״ן מֵ״ם רֵי״שׁ סוֹף as the initial letters — spells נְשָׁמָה, a hint to the additional soul (נְשָׁמָה יְתֵרָה) that every Israelite receives on the day of Shabbat. And according to our approach, this hints to the additional soul specifically in that command which came to Israel alone; but the nations have nothing but the נֶפֶשׁ alone, in the mystical scheme of רוּחַ נֶפֶשׁ נְשָׁמָה as is well known to those who know the science. Therefore the hint of the word נֶפֶשׁ comes through the word זָכוֹר, which is shared by both of them." "In the latter utterances it says ״כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוְּךָ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ regarding Shabbat and regarding the honor of father and mother. For at Marah Israel was commanded concerning them, but not the nations. And had He said this in the first Decalogue, it would have given the nations an opening to say: 'Why did He not also command us concerning these two commandments before the giving of the Torah?' And they would say: 'Just as He began to command Israel and not us, so too now we do not wish to receive, and with whomever He began, let Him finish.'" "In the first Decalogue He did not mention ״לְמַעַן יִיטַב לָךְ.״ For the Ibn Ezra explained that ״לְמַעַן יִיטַב לָךְ״ refers to the World to Come, which is entirely good, and ״לְמַעַן יַאֲרִיכוּן יָמֶיךָ״ refers to this world. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to promise the nations of the world in the first Decalogue anything but the reward of this world, whose reward a person sees with his own eyes, his labor evident before him; but had He promised them the reward of the World to Come, which no eye has seen, they surely would not have listened, for they would say 'one who wishes to lie distances his witnesses,' and they would say 'if this is not so, then that too is not so' — and they would be doubtful even about the reward of this world. But the latter Decalogue, which were spoken to Israel alone — He promised the reward of the World to Come, for they are believers, children of believers, and do not question HaShem, and believe in all His promises, whether in this world or in the World to Come." "In the first Decalogue He did not mention ״וְשׁוֹרְךָ וַחֲמֹרְךָ,״ for they are included in ״בְּהֶמְתֶּךָ.״ But in the latter Decalogue ״שׁוֹרְךָ וַחֲמֹרֶךָ״ is stated, to derive from them a גְּזֵרָה שָׁוָה: ״שׁוֹר שׁוֹר״ for muzzling (חֲסִימָה), ״חֲמוֹר חֲמוֹר״ for unloading (פְּרִיקָה) (Bava Kamma 54b), for Israel had already received all the utterances and the commandments of unloading and muzzling that were stated after the giving of the Torah. But the nations — before they heard the Ten Utterances, the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to test them first whether they would accept these utterances upon themselves; and had they accepted the utterances upon themselves, He would have afterward commanded them also the rest of the commandments, including muzzling and unloading as well. And therefore it was not for Him to write here ״וְשׁוֹרְךָ וַחֲמֹרְךָ״ for a גְּזֵרָה שָׁוָה, for all the commandments were still in doubt — whether they would accept them. But Israel said ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״ (Exodus 24:7) — that is, whatever He will yet command us." "In the first set He said 'false witness,' and in the latter set He added 'vain witness.' The Ramban explained that one must not testify even about a matter that entails no liability whatsoever — for example, if he testifies, 'So-and-so said to give this person a maneh,' and they did not acquire it from him forthwith. This is called שָׁוְא — a vain and void thing — and this is an extreme stringency. And this commandment is possible only for Israel, for 'the remnant of Israel shall do no iniquity nor speak lies' (Zephaniah 3:13), even though that false statement does no harm to one's fellow at all. And perhaps the nations could not stand up to this extreme stringency; therefore in the first set 'false witness' was stated. And the fact that 'witness' is said in the singular — it seems to me that one must not testify in it even as a single witness, for although a single witness does not impose monetary liability, none the less he establishes grounds for an oath. But when one testifies about a vain and void matter, it does not establish grounds even for an oath." "In the latter set He said 'You shall not covet' (לֹא תִתְאַוֶּה), in place of 'You shall not desire' (לֹא תַחְמֹד). You should know that the Ibn Ezra wrote that חֶמְדָּה can also be said of the robbery of a coveted thing, as it is written (Micah 2:2) 'וְחָמְדוּ שָׂדוֹת וְגָזָלוּ,' and it is written (Joshua 7:21) 'וָאֶחְמְדֵם וָאֶקָּחֵם.' And the Rambam agreed with him, saying that one does not transgress 'You shall not desire' until he takes the object that he desired; but תַּאֲוָה is nothing but in the heart alone. And the Ibn Ezra exerted himself to explain that there is power in a person's habituation to stand firm against the warning against desire and craving even in the heart — and this is when a person accustoms himself to the trait of contentment with what he has. And according to his words it emerges well that 'You shall not covet' (לֹא תִתְאַוֶּה) is stated specifically in the latter commandments, which belong to Israel alone, for they were habituated to the trait of contentment through the poverty of Egypt, and it was in their power to receive this commandment and fulfill it. But the nations, who were not habituated to the trait of contentment — how could they accept the commandment 'You shall not covet' (לֹא תִתְאַוֶּה)? For this matter is not in a person's hand to fulfill, for his heart is not delivered into his hand; rather, the wicked are in the domain of their own heart. Therefore He mentioned only חֶמְדָּה of robbery. And for this reason He did not mention 'his field' (שָׂדֵהוּ) in the first commandments, for bare land cannot be robbed and is always in the domain of its owner; but regarding craving that is in the heart, He mentioned also 'his field.'" "And the fact that in the latter set He used the language of חֶמְדָּה concerning 'your fellow's wife' and the language of תַּאֲוָה concerning 'his property' — it is all as a stringency: for concerning property He forbade even the craving of the heart, and all the more so חֶמְדָּה of robbery; but concerning a woman, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 29a) that thoughts of transgression are harsher than transgression. According to this, He used 'You shall not desire' (לֹא תַחְמֹד) as a stringency — all the more so the thought of the heart. And the nature of חִמּוּד of taking regarding a woman — that is, through his desiring her he contrives causes and devices so that her husband will divorce her and he will take her — this is surely not as bad as the constant thought. In the first set He placed 'house' before 'wife,' because desire for a house means to take it by robbery — that is, a prohibition that involves an act. But desire for a woman — the meaning is not that he may not desire to rob her while she is still under her husband, for if so that would be 'You shall not commit adultery'; rather, that he may not contrive against his fellow to divorce her so that he may take her — and this does not involve an act to the same degree. Therefore He said it by way of 'not only this but even that,' to say: not only desire for house and wealth to rob them — that is obviously forbidden — but even desire for his fellow's wife to take her after he divorces her, this too is forbidden even though it does not involve an act to the same degree. But in the latter set, where He used 'You shall not covet' (לֹא תִתְאַוֶּה) as a stringency — that concerning money even craving of the heart is forbidden, and concerning a woman He mentioned 'You shall not desire' (לֹא תַחְמֹד) — there is no 'not only this but even that' here, for desire and craving are two matters and they are equally weighted." "And the fact that in the latter commandments it is stated 'You shall not murder and you shall not commit adultery' — the וָי״ו adds to the first matter to forbid Israel even adultery by hand to emit seed in vain, for this adultery has an aspect of murder, namely the blood of his seed. As is derived in tractate Niddah (13a): Rabbi Elazar said, What is written (Isaiah 1:15) 'Your hands are full of blood'? These are those who commit adultery by hand. And so they expounded on this verse 'those who slaughter children in the valleys' (Isaiah 57:5) — even though they expounded (Niddah 13a): do not read 'those who slaughter' (שֹׁחֲטֵי) but 'those who press/squeeze' (סֹחֲטֵי), none the less one may also read in it 'those who slaughter' (שֹׁחֲטֵי) literally, for after all it is stated 'Your hands are full of blood.' And this is a warning specifically to Israel, for they are holy from the days of their fathers and are able to stand firm in this; but the first commandments, which were also stated to the nations, He did not wish to be so stringent upon them, for perhaps they would not be able to stand firm in this." "<b>And you shall not steal.</b> He hinted by means of the וָי״ו the wrapped/additional — that not only adultery with a married woman, but even adultery with an unmarried woman is forbidden, for it is called stolen waters, as it is written (Proverbs 9:17) 'מַיִם גְּנוּבִים יִמְתָּקוּ,' and this verse speaks of adultery with an unmarried woman." "<b>And you shall not answer against your fellow a false witness.</b> The vav adds to the first matter, to say: not only is theft of persons or of money forbidden, but even theft of the mind is forbidden — that one should not press upon him with excessive favors, knowing that he will not accept, and similar cases. And this falls under the category of false, vain, and pointless speech, and it is included in 'you shall not answer against your fellow a false witness.'" "<b>And you shall not covet.</b> The vav adds to the first matter, for everyone who steals the mind of others habitually flatters him, because he covets what belongs to his fellow and expects to receive from him some benefit on account of this; and if he does not covet, why should he flatter him for nothing? And all these extra stringencies — the nations could not have endured them, therefore HaShem did not wish to impose upon them all this severity and the like, and perhaps they would have listened. And when the nations did not wish to accept any of them, even the lighter ones, He returned and stated them to Israel along with all these stringencies, for He knew concerning them that their clay is pure and clean and that they are able to endure them. And through this all the difficulties and variant readings are resolved."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רס״ט in the Hebrew is resolved as 261 based on standard numerical values (ר=200, ס=60, ט=9), yielding a discourse number in his work עוללות אפרים.", 1 "The abbreviation מהרי״א is resolved as Mahariza; the specific scholar's full identity is uncertain from this passage alone.", 3 "The citation ילקוט דברים ל״ג תתקנ״א is normalized as Yalkut Shimoni, Deuteronomy 33, remez 951.", 4 "The Talmud citation סנהדרין נ״צ is resolved as Sanhedrin 58b. The abbreviation הקב״ה is rendered as 'the Holy One, blessed be He' per convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:43.657580463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}